---
import PostLayout from '../../layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import { POSTS } from '../../data/posts';
import { PRICING } from '../../data/brand';
const post = POSTS.find((p) => p.slug === 'what-0-percent-platform-fee-really-means')!;
---

<PostLayout post={post}>
  <p>
    Every ecommerce platform comparison eventually reaches the line "transaction fee". It is the most misread line in
    the table, because two very different charges hide behind the same words. This article separates them, then
    shows with a simple model why the platform's share matters more to a small store than to a large one.
  </p>

  <h2>Two fees, two companies</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>platform transaction fee</strong> is a percentage of each order that the store platform keeps for
    itself, on top of the subscription. The <strong>payment gateway fee</strong> is what PayPal, Stripe or another
    processor charges to move the money. The first is a platform policy and ranges from 0% to a few percent. The
    second exists on every platform and no store builder can remove it.
  </p>
  <p>
    So "0% platform transaction fee" means exactly one thing: the platform takes nothing per order. It does not mean
    "no fees". Your payment provider still charges its normal rate. See the guide
    <a href="/guides/platform-fee-vs-payment-gateway-fee">platform transaction fee vs payment gateway fee</a> for the
    full explanation.
  </p>

  <h2>A simple model</h2>
  <p>Take a small cross-border store that is still testing products:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>60 orders a month</li>
    <li>Average order 30 USD, so 1,800 USD of monthly sales</li>
    <li>Gateway fee the same on every platform, so it cancels out of the comparison</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Now compare two platform policies on that store:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Policy</th><th scope="col">Platform's share per month</th><th scope="col">Over 12 months</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">0% platform fee</th><td>0 USD</td><td>0 USD</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">1% platform fee</th><td>18 USD</td><td>216 USD</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">2% platform fee</th><td>36 USD</td><td>432 USD</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Those are small numbers, which is the point. For a large store, a 1–2% fee is a rounding error next to
    everything else it pays for. For a store doing 1,800 USD a month while testing, a 2% fee is comparable to a
    modest monthly subscription, and it is charged on top of one. The platform fee is a bigger share of a small
    seller's costs precisely because everything else is small too.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it matters most during testing</h2>
  <p>
    A product test usually runs on thin margins: ad spend is high relative to sales, and the goal is to learn, not
    to profit. A percentage taken from each test sale directly reduces the signal you get per dollar of ads. It also
    makes it harder to compare a platform's real cost against a marketplace's commission, because the platform is
    now taking a commission too.
  </p>

  <h2>What to write down when comparing</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Subscription price of the plan you would actually use.</li>
    <li>Platform transaction fee on that plan (0% or a number).</li>
    <li>Your gateway's rate, once, because it is roughly the same everywhere.</li>
    <li>Multiply by realistic monthly sales and add the subscription. Compare totals.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where Senvarico stands</h2>
  <p>
    Senvarico charges a {PRICING.platformFee} platform transaction fee on both the {PRICING.free.name} plan
    ({PRICING.free.priceLabel}, {PRICING.free.skuLabel}) and the {PRICING.paid.name} plan
    ({PRICING.paid.priceLabel}, {PRICING.paid.skuLabel}). PayPal and Stripe charge their own processing fees
    separately; Senvarico adds nothing on top. It is one option among several for small cross-border sellers, and the
    same arithmetic applies whichever platform you choose. See <a href="/pricing">pricing</a>.
  </p>
</PostLayout>
